Build **Arc Foundry**, an electro-industrial tower-defense game that runs entirely in
the browser — and **produce its art, effects, and audio yourself**, during this build,
with the asset-generation tools on your `PATH`.

Your project root is `{{workspace}}`. Create the game there.

## Your starting point

A starter project is already in place at `{{workspace}}`: a `package.json` that pins
**Playwright** as a dev dependency and depends on the
**`@test-cabinet/particle-runtime`** library (below). Its dependencies have already
been installed for you — `node_modules/` is populated and Playwright's headless
**Chromium** is downloaded — so you can build and verify right away. Build on this
project rather than replacing it: keep the `package.json`, add your framework,
bundler, and a `build` script on top of it, and keep the lockfile in sync (see
below).

## What to read first

The full specification is in the `specs/` directory at `{{workspace}}`, already
present in your project. Read all of it before you start, and produce an
implementation that matches the specification exactly. It is decomposed by concern:
`specs/overview.md` (the pitch and fiction, goals, hard requirements, the stage, the
palette and type, and the visual design), `specs/board.md` (the tile grid and uniform
component footprint, the **waypoint pathing and mazing model** — ordered waypoints,
shortest-open-route mazing, the never-seal rule, and live re-pathing — the **three
maps** with their exact waypoint coordinates, placement legality, and the HUD layout),
`specs/enemies.md` (the **Load** roster, the maze-ignoring flyer, the Dynamo boss, and
the per-wave HP scaling), `specs/towers.md` (the five **component types**, the
**quality ladder** and how it scales them, the stat tables, and the rotating head and
traveling-projectile rules), `specs/build.md` (the **scrap-press build loop** — the
on-placement random type/quality roll, the **keep exactly one per level** rule, inert
**blockers**, the combine recipe, and the **UPGRADE QUALITY** Refinement track),
`specs/flow.md` (the economy, Grid Integrity, the wave campaign, scoring, states,
menus, and HUD), `specs/modes.md` (the in-game **Easy/Medium/Hard difficulty** and the
map- and difficulty-select menus), `specs/controls.md`, `specs/assets.md` (the
asset-production contract), and `specs/proof.md`.

## Build requirements

Deliver a complete, polished, playable game, not a tech demo. The **hard
requirements** — real graphics, no backend or credentials, the palette and type, a
`README.md`, and the game states — are stated in full in `specs/overview.md` (and the
files it points to). That specification is authoritative; build to it rather than to
a summary here. Three things bear restating, because the harness depends on them:

- **The fixed build/serve interface.** The project stays a **Node project** built
  with only Node.js and npm-installed dependencies. Extend the provided
  `package.json` at `{{workspace}}` and **commit a `package-lock.json`** (run
  `npm install` after you change dependencies so the lockfile matches). `npm ci` then
  `npm run build` must produce the static site — with no other manual step — into one
  of `dist/`, `build/`, or `out/` at the project root, with an `index.html` at the
  root of that directory. Serving that directory as-is must run the game, and it must
  work **at any base path** (it is played back from a per-run sub-path, so any URL the
  build constructs at runtime must be page-relative — never a leading `/`; for a
  bundler, set a relative base such as `base: './'`). `specs/overview.md` and
  `specs/assets.md` state this in full; follow it exactly.
- **You PRODUCE the art, effects, and audio.** There is **no** pre-made game art in
  this project. Instead, the run image puts six asset-generation tools on your
  `PATH` — **`draw`** and **`draw-sheet`** (sprites and animated sprite sheets),
  **`particle-2d`** (particle systems), and **`sfx-synth`**, **`sfx-sample`**, and
  **`music`** (sound and music) — and you must **produce every asset the game plays
  with these tools during this build**, then wire the produced files into the game.
  Run each tool's `--help` to learn its operations. **`specs/assets.md` is the
  authoritative contract**: it enumerates every sprite, animation, particle system,
  and sound the game needs, names which tool produces each and where it lands under
  `assets/`, and states how each is wired into the build — including that the
  **electrical VFX** (the arcs, chain-lightning, spark showers, and discharges that
  are the headline of this build) are `particle-2d` systems played live through the
  **`@test-cabinet/particle-runtime`** library already installed as a dependency of
  your project (import it like any other dependency; do not fetch or reimplement it).
  Load every produced file with page-relative URLs so it resolves from the served
  sub-path.
- **The reference screenshots.** The `reference/` folder under `{{workspace}}` holds
  screenshots that are **illustrative examples, not targets to reproduce**, of the
  title, map-select, gameplay, and Overload screens — design your own menus and layout
  from the specification; the images only illustrate one way the intended look can
  come together, so build the screens from the specification rather than copying them.

## Verify before you finish

Your project has **Playwright** and a headless **Chromium** installed as project
dependencies, plus network access — use them to verify your build, do not just assume
it works. Before you consider the task complete:

- Produce the **static production build** and serve it locally, then open it in
  Chromium with Playwright and confirm it **loads with no console errors** and no
  uncaught exceptions.
- **Drive the game** — choose a map at the map select and a difficulty at the
  difficulty select, lay an opening maze in the untimed build phase, pull the
  scrap-press and place rocks that **roll a random component of a random quality on
  placement** (placing back-to-back without re-clicking STAMP, capped at five per
  level), **KEEP** exactly one roll as a firing tower and confirm the rest harden into
  inert **blockers** when the wave starts, **COMBINE** two matching rolls one tier up
  the quality ladder (and confirm the freed footprint re-paths the floor), buy an
  **UPGRADE QUALITY** level and see rolls bias upward, start a wave and watch the Load
  take the shortest **open** route around your maze between the map's 4-tile waypoint
  platforms, confirm a **Filament** flyer appears on a fourth wave and ignores the maze,
  reach a **Dynamo** milestone wave, and let the Load ground out at the Collector until
  Grid Integrity runs out — then reach both the Victory and the Overload screens — and
  **screenshot** the title, live-board, and Overload screens. Verify each against the
  specs — treating the `reference/` images as examples of the intended look only, not a
  layout to match — and fix what does not follow the specification before finishing.
- **Capture the proof of implementation** exactly as `specs/proof.md` specifies: the
  screenshots and the two short clips, each written to the precise `proof/` path that
  spec names. These are required deliverables of the finished build, not a private
  check. Do not invent a different folder or filename, and do not skip the `.webm`
  clips.

Use the project's own Playwright: import it from your project (for example
`import { chromium } from "playwright"`) or run the project-local CLI with
`npx playwright`. There is **no** global Playwright on `PATH` — it lives in your
project's `node_modules`, like any other dependency. Launch Chromium with the
`--no-sandbox` argument — this container has no Chromium sandbox, so a default launch
will fail.

## Commit your work

Your project root is a **git repository** — it was initialized before you started,
with the starter files in its first commit. Your finished implementation is published
directly from this repository's commits, so work you leave uncommitted is **not**
published. When the build is complete and verified, commit everything: run
`git add -A` then `git commit`, and confirm `git status` reports a clean working tree
with nothing uncommitted or untracked. A `.gitignore` already excludes `node_modules/`
and the build output (`dist/`/`build/`/`out/`), so a clean tree does not mean
committing those — but it does mean committing your source, your `package-lock.json`,
your `README.md`, the **produced assets under `assets/`**, and the `proof/` artifacts.
